/*@
\chapter{Introduction}

This document describes a compiler and run-time environment for a
statistical computing language. In syntax and semantics it is
substantially inspired by R \citep{ihaka96} -- lexical scope,
first-class functions, ad-hoc polymorphism of arithmetic and collection
subscript operators.

It includes an optimising compiler which performs type recovery,
procedure integration, constant folding, and dead code elimination. The
output code is interpreted by a directly threaded virtual machine.

It offers boolean, integer and double-precision floating-point scalar
data types in machine-native formats, with a distinguished ``missing''
value for each. The compiler produces efficient code for operations on
values of these types. Other types of object have reference semantics.

Unidimensional vectors and multidimensional arrays may contain values of
scalar or reference type, and may have optional symbolic names attached.


\section{Motivation}

The S programming language \citep{chambers84} was an early interactive
statistical computing system. The R language \citep{ihaka96}, itself
inspired by the later version of S \citep{chambers88}, is a significant
influence on this work. While popular and effective, some aspects of R's
design preclude reliable compilation for efficient execution.

\begin{itemize}
\item Variables, function arguments and return values may take on values
  of any type, at any point in the program.
\item Scalars are represented as vectors of length one.
\item Call-by-value behaviour requires expensive copying, in the
  presence of subscript assignment.
\item Reflective manipulation of the run-time environment can invalidate
  any static properties a compiler may have been able to deduce.
\end{itemize}

The system described in this document adopts much of R's syntax and some
of its semantics. Others are included subject to minor constraints, or
omitted entirely.

\begin{itemize}
\item Type declarations, and annotations for expressing that certain
  global variables have constant values.
\item Values of scalar type, distinct from vectors.
\item Call-by-reference for collections. Copying across function
  invocation may be explicitly requested by the user, if desired.
\item Strictly lexical variable access. Local variables are only ever
  local; each may only be referred to within a single block of source
  code.
\end{itemize}

These changes are made in service of an optimising compiler: drawing on
a selection of powerful algorithms from the computer science literature,
it transforms a user's program to improve its efficiency while retaining
its meaning.

A primary goal of this work is to expand the kinds of code that users
can write without resorting to built-in functions for reasons of
performance.

